      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bristol was named after Bristol, England, and founded in 1680 as a deep‑water seaport.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.explorebristolri.com/history-of-bristol"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Bristol Historical & Preservation Society, housed in the 1828 Bristol County Jail, preserves the town’s maritime past.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.explorebristolri.com/history-of-bristol"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "King Philip's War began in nearby Swansea in 1675, a pivotal moment for Bristol’s early settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.explorebristolri.com/history-of-bristol"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town celebrates America’s oldest Fourth of July parade, a tradition dating back to 1765.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.explorebristolri.com/history-of-bristol"
    },
    {
